WEBVTT

NOTE
Timings are measured from the synthesised narration by narrate.py. After the
edit, fix the times here and copy them across to 02-captions.ja.vtt — the cue
numbering is the same in both files, so only the timestamps move.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.221
This is audio.

2
00:00:01.472 --> 00:00:04.091
It swings above and below zero volts.

3
00:00:04.341 --> 00:00:07.403
Last time, a bare Pico could not see half of it.

4
00:00:07.653 --> 00:00:09.109
This is what changed —

5
00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:12.747
one op amp, and a few parts that cost less than the cable.

NOTE section

6
00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:19.008
A Pico's converter reads zero to three point three volts.

7
00:00:19.259 --> 00:00:22.544
Audio, a function generator, anything alternating —

8
00:00:22.795 --> 00:00:26.757
they spend half their time below zero, where the converter sees nothing.

9
00:00:27.008 --> 00:00:29.840
And there's a second problem nobody warns you about.

10
00:00:30.091 --> 00:00:34.949
The converter doesn't just read a voltage; it takes a sip of charge every time it samples.

11
00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:38.224
Anything that can't supply that sip quickly reads low.

12
00:00:38.475 --> 00:00:40.443
So a front end has two jobs.

13
00:00:40.693 --> 00:00:45.136
Give the converter something stiff to drink from, and put the signal where it can see it.

14
00:00:45.387 --> 00:00:49.653
One op amp has two halves. Each half does one job.

NOTE section

15
00:00:52.107 --> 00:00:58.485
An MCP6022. It's a dual, so there are two amplifiers in the one package.

16
00:00:58.736 --> 00:01:04.384
The first is a follower: the input goes straight in, and the output copies it. Gain of one.

17
00:01:04.635 --> 00:01:09.045
It changes nothing about the voltage — what it changes is who does the work.

18
00:01:09.296 --> 00:01:15.653
Whatever you're measuring drives a few picoamps into the op amp, and the op amp drives the converter.

19
00:01:15.904 --> 00:01:21.520
The second half does the same, but its input comes through a capacitor and two hundred-kilohm resistors.

20
00:01:21.771 --> 00:01:26.213
The resistors hold that point at mid rail — one point six five volts —

21
00:01:26.464 --> 00:01:28.992
and the capacitor lets only the swing through.

22
00:01:29.243 --> 00:01:35.035
So a signal centred on zero comes out centred on one point six five, and the whole of it fits.

23
00:01:35.285 --> 00:01:39.291
On each output there's a kilohm and four point seven nanofarads.

24
00:01:39.541 --> 00:01:43.696
That's not an anti-alias filter; one pole isn't enough for that.

25
00:01:43.946 --> 00:01:52.160
It's there so the converter's sampling capacitor never hangs directly on an op amp's output — the kind of load that makes some amplifiers ring.

26
00:01:52.410 --> 00:01:54.203
That is the whole parts list.

NOTE section

27
00:01:56.656 --> 00:01:58.437
Here's the sip, made visible.

28
00:01:58.688 --> 00:02:03.466
A divider of two hundred-kilohm resistors sits at one point six five volts.

29
00:02:03.717 --> 00:02:10.330
Through the follower, the instrument reads one point six four, and it sits there — eighteen millivolts peak to peak.

30
00:02:10.581 --> 00:02:15.002
Straight into the converter, the same node reads the same one point six four —

31
00:02:15.253 --> 00:02:20.282
but it's not still. Eighty-three millivolts peak to peak, more than four times as much.

32
00:02:20.533 --> 00:02:22.938
The mean survives; the stillness doesn't.

33
00:02:23.189 --> 00:02:29.194
The converter takes a sip of charge every time it samples, and fifty kilohms can't put it back before the next one —

34
00:02:29.445 --> 00:02:33.893
so the reading is right on average, and wrong on every individual sample.

35
00:02:34.144 --> 00:02:38.213
The converter wants whatever feeds it to look like less than ten kilohms.

36
00:02:38.464 --> 00:02:42.480
A follower looks like almost nothing, and never runs out of charge.

NOTE section

37
00:02:44.933 --> 00:02:50.602
The first op amp in that socket was an LM358, because every drawer has one.

38
00:02:50.853 --> 00:02:56.496
And the instrument showed me the problem immediately. Look at the top of the trace. Flat.

39
00:02:56.746 --> 00:03:02.640
An LM358's output can't get closer than about one and a half volts to its positive supply.

40
00:03:02.890 --> 00:03:07.237
On three point three volts, that means it stops near one point eight.

41
00:03:07.488 --> 00:03:12.304
Everything above that is gone — and one point eight is barely past the middle of the range.

42
00:03:12.554 --> 00:03:17.578
The MCP6022 is rail to rail, input and output.

43
00:03:17.829 --> 00:03:23.749
Driven with the generator's raw carrier, its output swung three point one five volts peak to peak —

44
00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:28.693
within seventy-five millivolts of both rails, on a three point three volt supply.

45
00:03:28.944 --> 00:03:32.576
The part number is the one choice in this circuit that isn't free.

46
00:03:32.826 --> 00:03:37.210
If you substitute, the one thing to check is those two words: rail to rail.

NOTE section

47
00:03:39.664 --> 00:03:41.872
Now the Mac's own headphone jack.

48
00:03:42.122 --> 00:03:46.949
Its output swings about zero, and through the second half it arrives centred on mid rail.

49
00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:49.909
One detail, because it's the kind of thing that bites.

50
00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:53.637
This jack decides how hard to drive by how much it's loaded.

51
00:03:53.888 --> 00:03:59.530
Apple's own published ceilings: under a hundred fifty ohms, one and a quarter volts RMS;

52
00:03:59.781 --> 00:04:04.464
from there to a kilohm, three volts RMS — already past the converter's whole range.

53
00:04:04.714 --> 00:04:11.445
Left to itself, looking at fifty kilohms, this channel sits past both brackets, driven as a line input.

54
00:04:11.696 --> 00:04:16.677
A hundred ohms across the plug drops it under a hundred fifty ohms, headphone territory,

55
00:04:16.928 --> 00:04:23.546
and the music we actually play into it stays well under that ceiling — it lands inside the range with room to spare.

56
00:04:23.797 --> 00:04:26.890
And this is where last episode's decision pays off.

57
00:04:27.141 --> 00:04:31.904
The reading is still the volts the converter saw — one point six five plus the audio.

58
00:04:32.154 --> 00:04:38.197
I tell the panel the bias is at mid rail, it draws the dotted line there, and zero is wherever the line is.

59
00:04:38.448 --> 00:04:41.018
Nothing has been subtracted behind your back.

60
00:04:41.269 --> 00:04:45.173
Two hundred-kilohm resistors look like fifty kilohms to the signal.

61
00:04:45.424 --> 00:04:50.000
With one and a half microfarads, anything above about two hertz gets through.

62
00:04:50.250 --> 00:04:58.837
Fifty kilohms is also the price. A function generator or a headphone output doesn't notice it; a sensor or a high-value divider would.

63
00:04:59.088 --> 00:05:05.141
So high-impedance things go on the first channel, the plain follower, where the load is picoamps.

64
00:05:05.392 --> 00:05:09.685
That value wasn't my first choice, either. It started at a megohm a side,

65
00:05:09.936 --> 00:05:15.050
and the AC channel went soft from about ten kilohertz, while the direct one didn't.

66
00:05:15.301 --> 00:05:18.304
At a hundred kilohms, the two channels fall off together.

NOTE section

67
00:05:20.757 --> 00:05:25.013
Both of these happened to me while making this, and the screen said so both times.

68
00:05:25.264 --> 00:05:30.128
They're worth two minutes because the symptom is identical, and it's not the symptom you'd guess.

69
00:05:30.378 --> 00:05:33.301
The first one: the audio went into the direct channel.

70
00:05:33.552 --> 00:05:38.213
That half has no capacitor and no divider — it's a follower and nothing else —

71
00:05:38.464 --> 00:05:43.616
so a signal centred on zero arrives centred on zero, and everything below the line is gone.

72
00:05:43.866 --> 00:05:46.906
The second one is better, because the circuit was right.

73
00:05:47.157 --> 00:05:52.976
The hundred ohms that tells the jack it's driving headphones had gone in on the wrong side of the coupling capacitor —

74
00:05:53.226 --> 00:05:56.517
across the op amp's input instead of across the plug.

75
00:05:56.768 --> 00:06:02.704
A hundred ohms against the divider's fifty kilohms wins, and it pulls mid rail down to nothing.

76
00:06:02.954 --> 00:06:05.306
Same picture. Bottom half gone.

77
00:06:05.557 --> 00:06:10.154
And that's how you tell them apart without a meter: unplug the source and watch the bias.

78
00:06:10.405 --> 00:06:17.775
If it comes back to one point six five and collapses when you plug in, the source is dragging the bias, not the circuit failing to make it.

79
00:06:18.026 --> 00:06:21.285
Put it back at the plug and the picture is what it should be:

80
00:06:21.535 --> 00:06:25.632
centred on the line, a volt and a half of swing, and room for more.

81
00:06:25.882 --> 00:06:30.799
The screen never told me what was wrong. It told me what it saw — which was enough, twice.

NOTE section

82
00:06:33.253 --> 00:06:38.362
Brown, black, red is one kilohm. Brown, black, yellow is a hundred.

83
00:06:38.613 --> 00:06:45.989
The position is the generator's own RC, out on the breadboard between the PWM pin and the mini-AFE's input.

84
00:06:46.239 --> 00:06:49.626
It was meant to be a kilohm. I fitted a hundred kilohms.

85
00:06:49.877 --> 00:06:55.423
Everything still worked — the trace was there, it had the right shape — it was just smaller than it should be,

86
00:06:55.674 --> 00:06:58.309
and I spent an afternoon doubting the op amp.

87
00:06:58.559 --> 00:07:06.762
What caught it wasn't a meter. It was the instrument, reading the response at a few frequencies, and the numbers refusing to match the arithmetic.

88
00:07:07.013 --> 00:07:13.845
It happened twice. A capacitor marked one-oh-four — a hundred nanofarads — had gone in where a one-oh-three belonged.

89
00:07:14.095 --> 00:07:17.482
Same symptom: the five kilohertz reading came up short.

90
00:07:17.733 --> 00:07:24.111
With the right parts, the generator's sine through its filter reads three point one five volts at four forty,

91
00:07:24.362 --> 00:07:29.130
three point one two at one kilohertz, and two point eight eight at five.

92
00:07:29.381 --> 00:07:36.314
The lesson is the same as last time: when the number disagrees with the arithmetic, believe the number, then go and find out why.

NOTE section

93
00:07:38.767 --> 00:07:39.850
It has no protection.

94
00:07:40.101 --> 00:07:44.095
There's nothing in front of the op amp but a capacitor, or nothing at all,

95
00:07:44.346 --> 00:07:53.242
so keep the input inside the supply — zero to three point three on the direct channel, and about plus or minus one and a half volts on the AC one.

96
00:07:53.493 --> 00:07:58.495
It has no attenuation. Gain is one, so the range is still the converter's range.

97
00:07:58.746 --> 00:08:05.274
And that's fine. It's a breadboard and an evening, and it turns a Pico that can't see audio into one that can.

98
00:08:05.525 --> 00:08:11.221
If you'd rather try it before you wire it, the whole circuit runs in a browser simulator — the link is below.

NOTE section

99
00:08:13.674 --> 00:08:19.253
Next: a front end that's protected, switches ranges, and reads plus or minus twenty-five volts —

100
00:08:19.503 --> 00:08:23.253
and a board someone else designed, with calibration to prove on camera.

101
00:08:23.503 --> 00:08:27.002
The circuit, the parts list and the simulator link are below.

102
00:08:27.253 --> 00:08:29.738
If you built the last one, this is the evening after.
